# spark-ops.env.example — OPTIONAL local overrides for the Spark ops routines.
#
# You normally DON'T need this file. By default the alert layer (ops/_common.sh
# notify()) delivers via **Pushover**, resolving the app token + user key from
# **Bitwarden Secrets Manager** at runtime (items PUSHOVER_APP_TOKEN and
# PUSHOVER_USER_KEY), using the BWS access token from ~/.config/claude-env.sh.
# Nothing secret is written to disk. This matches the "tokens in Bitwarden,
# never in .env files" rule.
#
# Copy this to ONE of these (both gitignored / outside the public repo) ONLY if
# you want to override the defaults — ops/_common.sh sources it at runtime:
#   ~/.spark-ops.env            (preferred — outside the repo)
#   ops/spark-ops.env           (repo-local; gitignored)
# Use `:=` assignment so a value already exported in the environment still wins.

# --- Option 1: pin different Bitwarden item names (still zero secrets on disk) ---
# : "${SPARK_BWS_PUSHOVER_TOKEN_KEY:=PUSHOVER_APP_TOKEN}"
# : "${SPARK_BWS_PUSHOVER_USER_KEY:=PUSHOVER_USER_KEY}"

# --- Option 2: cache the Pushover creds locally to drop the runtime Bitwarden
#     dependency (more robust for headless cron, but writes the app token to this
#     mode-600 file). Only do this deliberately. ---
# : "${SPARK_PUSHOVER_TOKEN:=your-pushover-app-token}"
# : "${SPARK_PUSHOVER_USER:=your-pushover-user-key}"
# export SPARK_PUSHOVER_TOKEN SPARK_PUSHOVER_USER

# --- Option 3: generic raw-body webhook fallback (ntfy/Slack/Discord). Only used
#     if no Pushover creds resolve. ---
# : "${SPARK_ALERT_WEBHOOK:=https://ntfy.sh/your-topic}"
# export SPARK_ALERT_WEBHOOK

# Optional email (requires a working mailer, e.g. msmtp/sendmail, on this host):
# MAILTO="you@example.com"
